 ## What the clove experiment actually compared
 
-The researchers extracted clove buds with 80% methanol, concentrated the extract and freeze-dried it before encapsulation.[1] They prepared six wall-material blends of maltodextrin (MD, dextrose equivalent 16.5–19.5) and gum arabic (GA), used a stated extract-to-wall-material ratio of 1:4, homogenized the mixtures, froze them at −80 °C and freeze-dried them for about 72 hours.[1] The MD:GA labels describe the carrier blend, not the composition of undiluted clove extract.
+The researchers extracted clove buds with 80% methanol, concentrated the extract and freeze-dried it before encapsulation.[1] They prepared six wall-material blends of maltodextrin (MD, dextrose equivalent 16.5–19.5) and gum arabic (GA), mixed freeze-dried extract with a 30% w/v wall-material solution at a stated ratio of 1:4, homogenized the mixtures, froze them at −80 °C and freeze-dried them for about 72 hours.[1] The MD:GA labels describe the carrier blend, not the composition of undiluted clove extract. The method does not specify the basis of the 1:4 ratio; it should not be read as a verified dry extract-to-carrier mass ratio.[1]
 
 These were laboratory freeze-dried extract powders—not ground cloves, distilled clove oil or a demonstrated commercial spray-dried ingredient.[1] The methanolic extraction route also means the experiment should not be treated as a ready-to-use food-production specification.
 
